# Line endings, declared once, so a merge is a merge and not a whole-file rewrite.
#
# ⛔ THE DEFECT THIS REMOVES. There was no .gitattributes in this repository and
# `core.autocrlf` is `false`, so git stored whatever bytes each lane's tooling happened to
# write. On 2026-08-31 that turned FIVE OF SEVEN merge conflicts into WHOLE-FILE conflicts:
# one store presented as a 2,000-line rewrite of a 2,000-line file, and with whitespace
# ignored the real change was 144 lines.
#
# ⚠️ AND THE DANGER IS NOT THE NOISE, IT IS WHAT THE NOISE INVITES. When every line of a
# file conflicts, "take ours" or "take theirs" is the only tractable move -- and either one
# SILENTLY DELETES THE OTHER LANE'S WORK WHILE LOOKING LIKE A DELIBERATE RESOLUTION. The
# diff cannot show you what you are discarding, so the destruction is invisible at exactly
# the moment a human is deciding.
#
# MEASURED BEFORE WRITING THIS, over 13,710 tracked files:
#
#     LF     10,255      CRLF    1,666      MIXED   279      no newline  1,343
#     BINARY    167
#
#     .json   4,659    LF 4,446   CRLF   165   MIXED   1
#     .html   2,084    LF   773   CRLF 1,228   MIXED  81   <- THE SPLIT CLASS
#     .md     1,473    LF 1,450   CRLF    23
#     .py     1,205    LF 1,074   CRLF   130   MIXED   1
#     .out      898    LF   604   CRLF     1   MIXED 190   <- captured output, left alone
#
# `.html` is both the biggest conflict source and the deliverable, and `build_tabbed.py`
# was itself mixed at 1,902 CRLF against 104 LF. LF is chosen because it is already the
# majority everywhere except the pages.
#
# INCLUDING `.html` IS DELIBERATE, AND THE INSTINCT RUNS THE OTHER WAY. It is the
# deliverable, so the reflex is to leave it alone -- but 1,228 CRLF against 773 LF is
# precisely WHERE THE CONFLICTS ARE, and excluding it would dodge the problem this file
# exists to solve while looking like the cautious choice. Two facts make it safe: the pages
# are GENERATED from objects by build_tabbed.py rather than hand-edited, so a normalised
# page is a rebuild away; and nothing hashes page bytes -- artefact_registry scans
# `scripts/`, `.githooks/`, `ssot/` generators and the repository root, and does not
# register the reviews.

* text=auto eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EXPLICITLY NOT NORMALISED, and the reason matters more than the list.
#
# `scripts/artefact_registry.py::sha(path)` hashes FILE BYTES ON DISK, over `scripts/`,
# `.githooks/`, `ssot/` generators and the repository root. Anything whose recorded identity
# is a hash of its bytes must not have those bytes rewritten underneath the record, or the
# hash stops identifying the thing it names -- which is the provenance defect this project
# has spent a night removing, arriving through the back door of a whitespace setting.
#
# Captured output (.out, .task) is EVIDENCE OF A RUN, not source. 190 of 898 `.out` files
# are mixed because that is what the process emitted; normalising them would edit the
# record of what happened.
*.out       -text
*.task      -text
*.log       -text

# Binary, by content rather than by hope. 167 tracked files contain NUL bytes.
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.gif       binary
*.ico       binary
*.pdf       binary
*.zip       binary
*.gz        binary
*.duckdb    binary
*.woff      binary
*.woff2     binary
*.ttf       binary
*.eps       binary
*.tiff      binary
